APXF["za-paardeberg-perdeberg"] = {"lede": "The granite massif of the Paardeberg (Perdeberg in Afrikaans), on the border of the Swartland and Paarl, is the spiritual home of the Swartland revival.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Chenin Blanc, Shiraz, Grenache, Cinsault, Mourvèdre"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Eben Sadie's farm and cellar, A.A. Badenhorst's Kalmoesfontein and many old Chenin and Cinsault vineyards are here. Its decomposed granite holds winter rain, so old bush vines survive without irrigation.</p><p>Decomposed granite over clay on rounded hills; warm, dry and windy.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Old-vine Chenin Blanc and white blends; Syrah, Grenache, Cinsault and Rhône blends, unforced and fresh.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Swartland district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": ["Sadie Family", "A.A. Badenhorst", "Perdeberg Wines", "Lammershoek (nearby)"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-paardeberg-south"] = {"lede": "The southern foothills of the Paardeberg, separated as their own ward, with granite soils similar to the main Paardeberg ward.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Chenin Blanc, Shiraz, Pinotage"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Old dry-farmed bush vines of Chenin Blanc and Cinsault here supply several new-wave Swartland labels.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Old-vine Chenin Blanc and white blends; Syrah, Grenache, Cinsault and Rhône blends, unforced and fresh.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Swartland district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": [], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-paarl"] = {"lede": "Paarl, named after the pearl-like granite domes of Paarl Mountain, lies north of Stellenbosch along the Berg River.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Pinotage, Chenin Blanc, Chardonnay"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It was the seat of the KWV from 1918, which made it the administrative capital of the industry for most of the twentieth century, and home to Nederburg, whose annual auction (from 1975) became the country's most important wine sale. The district is warmer than Stellenbosch and its Shiraz, Cabernet, Pinotage and Chenin are generous and ripe. Its wards are Simonsberg-Paarl, Voor-Paardeberg and Agter-Paarl.</p><p>Granite domes and slopes, with alluvial soils along the Berg River; the valley needs supplementary irrigation in hot summers while the eastern slopes hold water.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Paarl takes its name from the granite domes of Paarl Mountain, which glisten like pearls after rain. The KWV, founded here in 1918, was headquartered in the town and ran the industry until the 1990s; its Cathedral Cellar holds enormous old oak vats. Fairview, Nederburg and the Afrikaans Language Monument are nearby.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Full Shiraz, Pinotage, Chenin Blanc and Rhône-style blends.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Granite domes; KWV headquarters (1918); Nederburg Auction.</p>"]], "producers": ["Nederburg", "KWV", "Fairview", "Glen Carlou", "Backsberg", "Avondale", "Rupert & Rothschild", "Plaisir de Merle"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-papegaaiberg"] = {"lede": "A small hill just west of Stellenbosch town, the 'parrot mountain' where early settlers practised shooting at a wooden parrot.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Chenin Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>The Bergkelder, Distell's (now Heineken Beverages') maturation cellar, is tunnelled into its slopes.</p><p>Granite and shale on a low ridge.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Structured Cabernet Sauvignon and Bordeaux blends, Pinotage, Chenin Blanc and Chardonnay.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Stellenbosch district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": ["Bergkelder"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-philadelphia"] = {"lede": "A small ward north of Durbanville around the village of Philadelphia, on hills up to about 260 m within sight of the Atlantic, known for structured Bordeaux-style reds.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Capaia, with its hilltop winery, is the ward's best-known estate.</p><p>Weathered shale and granite; cool Atlantic influence with large day-night differences.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Cool Sauvignon Blanc, Semillon and Bordeaux blends; Constantia's sweet Muscat.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Cape Town district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": ["Capaia", "Havana Hills"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-piket-bo-berg"] = {"lede": "A high plateau on top of the Piketberg mountain, north of the Swartland at about 600 to 700 m, cooler than the valleys below; the WOSA text calls it Piketberg-Bo-Berg.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Chenin Blanc, Sauvignon Blanc, Shiraz"]], "sections": [["The place", ""], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Cabernet, Pinotage, Shiraz, Chenin Blanc and Sauvignon Blanc.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>WO unit, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": [], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-polkadraai-hills"] = {"lede": "Hills west of Stellenbosch near the R310 and Polkadraai Road, open to cool breezes from both False Bay and the Atlantic.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Chenin Blanc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Johan Reyneke farms biodynamically here, and the ward's sandy granite soils give fresh, fragrant Syrah and Chenin.</p><p>Sandy, decomposed granite over clay at about 100 to 300 m.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Structured Cabernet Sauvignon and Bordeaux blends, Pinotage, Chenin Blanc and Chardonnay.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Stellenbosch district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": ["Reyneke", "Stellenbosch Hills", "Mooiplaas (nearby)"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-porseleinberg"] = {"lede": "A ward named after the 'porcelain mountain' between Riebeek-Kasteel and the Berg River, whose blue-grey schist soils Boekenhoutskloof chose for its Porseleinberg Syrah, farmed organically by Callie Louw since the first vintage in 2010.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Syrah"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Malmesbury schist with very little topsoil; warm and dry.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Old-vine Chenin Blanc and white blends; Syrah, Grenache, Cinsault and Rhône blends, unforced and fresh.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Swartland district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": ["Porseleinberg (Boekenhoutskloof)"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-riebeekberg"] = {"lede": "The slopes of the Kasteelberg around the villages of Riebeek-Kasteel and Riebeek-West, where the Mullineuxs farm their Roundstone vineyard on schist and iron-rich soils.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Chenin Blanc, Grenache, Mourvèdre"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Many of the Swartland's new-wave producers source fruit here.</p><p>Schist, shale and koffieklip (iron-rich ferricrete).</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Old-vine Chenin Blanc and white blends; Syrah, Grenache, Cinsault and Rhône blends, unforced and fresh.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Swartland district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": ["Mullineux", "Kloovenburg", "Riebeek Cellars"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-riebeeksrivier"] = {"lede": "A newer ward along the Riebeeks River valley below the Kasteelberg, on shale and alluvial soils.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Chenin Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It lies between the Riebeekberg and Porseleinberg wards in the heart of the new-wave Swartland.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Old-vine Chenin Blanc and white blends; Syrah, Grenache, Cinsault and Rhône blends, unforced and fresh.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Swartland district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": [], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-simonsberg-paarl"] = {"lede": "The north-eastern slopes of the Simonsberg facing Paarl, on decomposed granite.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Chardonnay, Merlot"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Glen Carlou, Plaisir de Merle, Backsberg and Rupert & Rothschild make Bordeaux blends and Chardonnay here. It is warmer than the Simonsberg-Stellenbosch side of the mountain, and its reds are correspondingly riper.</p><p>Granite-derived soils on north- and east-facing slopes; warmer than the Stellenbosch side of the mountain.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Full Shiraz, Pinotage, Chenin Blanc and Rhône-style blends.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Paarl district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": ["Glen Carlou", "Plaisir de Merle", "Backsberg", "Rupert & Rothschild", "Noble Hill"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-simonsberg-stellenbosch"] = {"lede": "The southern and western slopes of the granite Simonsberg (1,390 m) north-east of Stellenbosch, one of the most prestigious wards in South Africa and among the first to be demarcated.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinotage, Merlot, Shiraz, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Its well-drained, red decomposed-granite soils, altitude and afternoon sea breezes give structured, long-lived Cabernet and Bordeaux blends and the country's most famous Pinotage. Kanonkop, Rustenberg, Thelema, Tokara, Warwick, Delheim and Uitkyk are all here.</p><p>Decomposed granite (red Hutton and Clovelly soils) at about 150 to 500 m; south- and west-facing slopes catch the False Bay breeze.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Structured Cabernet Sauvignon and Bordeaux blends, Pinotage, Chenin Blanc and Chardonnay.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Stellenbosch ward; granite Simonsberg; Kanonkop, Rustenberg, Thelema; Cabernet and Pinotage.</p>"]], "producers": ["Kanonkop", "Rustenberg", "Thelema", "Tokara", "Warwick", "Delheim", "Uitkyk", "Morgenhof", "Le Bonheur"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-stellenbosch"] = {"lede": "Stellenbosch is South Africa's most famous wine district and its intellectual centre.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", "1973 (Wine of Origin scheme)"], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, Merlot, Pinotage, Chenin Blanc, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>The town, founded by Simon van der Stel in 1679 and shaded by oaks, has the country's only university degree in viticulture and oenology, the Elsenburg agricultural college and the Nietvoorbij research institute. More than 200 producers farm the district's slopes, from historic estates to modern wineries.</p><p>Its strength is red wine, above all Cabernet Sauvignon and Bordeaux-style blends, which gain structure and freshness from the district's granite mountains and the cooling south-easter off False Bay. Meerlust Rubicon (first made in 1980), Kanonkop Paul Sauer, Vergelegen V and Rust en Vrede's estate blend are classics, and Kanonkop's Pinotage, from vines planted in the 1950s, is the reference for the country's own grape. Old-vine Chenin Blanc from the Bottelary and Helderberg areas and Chardonnay from the higher slopes are equally serious.</p><p>The district is divided into eight wards: Banghoek, Bottelary, Devon Valley, Jonkershoek Valley, Papegaaiberg, Polkadraai Hills, Simonsberg-Stellenbosch and Vlottenburg. Much of the best-known land, including the Helderberg slopes around Somerset West (Vergelegen, Rust en Vrede, Waterford, Ernie Els), lies outside any ward and is labelled simply 'Stellenbosch'. The Stellenbosch Wine Route, founded in 1971, was South Africa's first.</p><p>Mountains on three sides (Simonsberg, Jonkershoek, Helderberg) and False Bay to the south; about 600 to 800 mm of rain in the valley, more on the mountains. Decomposed granite on the higher slopes, Table Mountain sandstone higher still, and Malmesbury shale and alluvium on the lower ground to the west.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Founded by Simon van der Stel in 1679, Stellenbosch is South Africa's second-oldest town and the seat of its wine university and research institute. Professor Abraham Perold crossed Pinot Noir with Cinsaut (then called Hermitage) at Stellenbosch University in 1925 to create Pinotage, and the first commercial Pinotage was released in 1961 from the 1959 Lanzerac vintage. In 1971 the Stellenbosch Wine Route, the first in the country, opened. Its granite mountains, Simonsberg, Helderberg and Jonkershoek, and False Bay breezes make it the home of the Cape's great Cabernet and Bordeaux-style blends from estates such as Kanonkop, Meerlust, Rustenberg and Vergelegen.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Cabernet, Pinotage, Shiraz, Chenin Blanc and Sauvignon Blanc.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>1679; Pinotage (Perold 1925); first Pinotage release 1961; Wine Route 1971; Cabernet and blends.</p>"]], "producers": ["Kanonkop", "Meerlust", "Rustenberg", "Vergelegen", "Rust en Vrede", "Thelema", "Tokara", "Warwick", "Delheim", "Jordan", "Waterford", "De Toren", "Ken Forrester", "Kleine Zalze", "Simonsig", "Reyneke", "Alheit (sources)", "Mvemve Raats", "Raats", "Keermont", "Le Riche"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-swartland"] = {"lede": "The Swartland, the 'black land', takes its name from the renosterbos (rhino bush) that once darkened its rolling wheatfields north of Cape Town.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Chenin Blanc, Shiraz, Grenache, Cinsault, Pinotage, Mourvèdre, Palomino"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>For most of its history it grew grain and supplied big co-operatives with high-yielding grapes. That changed when Charles Back of Fairview and Spice Route began buying land here in the late 1990s and a young winemaker, Eben Sadie, found old, dry-farmed bush vines of Chenin Blanc, Grenache, Cinsault and Syrah on the Paardeberg. His Columella (2000) and Palladius (2002) showed what they could do.</p><p>A generation followed: Chris and Andrea Mullineux, Adi Badenhorst at Kalmoesfontein, Callie Louw at Porseleinberg, Craig Hawkins (Testalonga), Lammershoek, David and Nadia Sadie, Donovan Rall. Their Swartland Revolution festival (2010 to 2015) and the Swartland Independent Producers association, which sets rules on natural winemaking and regional sourcing, made the district shorthand for South Africa's new wave: whole-bunch Syrah, old-vine Chenin blends aged in old foudres and concrete, and skin-contact whites. The district also has eight wards, from the Paardeberg granite to the schist of the Riebeek and Porseleinberg slopes.</p><p>Warm and dry (400 to 600 mm of rain), with mostly dry-farmed bush vines. Soils include decomposed granite (Paardeberg), Malmesbury shale and schist (Kasteelberg, Porseleinberg) and iron-rich koffieklip.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style.</p>"], ["History", "<p>The Swartland, named after the dark renosterbos that covers its hills, was wheat and cooperative bulk-wine country. Charles Back and the Spice Route winery came in the late 1990s, Eben Sadie made his first Columella in 2000, and Chris and Andrea Mullineux, Adi Badenhorst and others followed, working dry-farmed old bush vines of Chenin Blanc, Cinsault and Grenache with minimal intervention. In 2011 they formed the Swartland Independent Producers, with rules on natural winemaking, and the 'Swartland Revolution' made the region the most exciting in the Cape. Rosa Kruger, the viticulturist who scouted many of these old vineyards, founded the Old Vine Project, whose Certified Heritage Vineyard seal shows the planting date of vines at least 35 years old.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Old-vine Chenin Blanc and white blends; Syrah, Grenache, Cinsault and Rhône blends, unforced and fresh.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Renosterbos; Sadie (Columella 2000); Mullineux; Swartland Independent Producers 2011; old bush vines; Old Vine Project.</p>"]], "producers": ["Sadie Family", "Mullineux", "A.A. Badenhorst", "Porseleinberg", "Lammershoek", "David & Nadia", "Testalonga", "Rall", "Spice Route", "Swartland Winery", "Kloovenburg"], "sources": [["Old Vine Project: Certified Heritage Vineyards", "https://www.oldvineproject.co.za/"], ["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-tulbagh"] = {"lede": "A horseshoe-shaped valley enclosed by the Groot Winterhoek, Witzenberg and Obiekwaberg mountains, about 90 minutes north of Cape Town.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Chenin Blanc, Mourvèdre, Pinotage"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Cold air pools on the valley floor at night (a 'cold trap'), giving large day-night temperature swings. Tulbagh's Church Street has 32 national monuments, many restored after the 1969 earthquake. The valley is known for Shiraz and Rhône blends and for Cap Classique pioneer Krone (Twee Jonge Gezellen).</p><p>A former lake basin with varied alluvial and sandstone soils.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style.</p>"], ["History", "<p>A horseshoe valley under the Witzenberg and Groot Winterhoek mountains, Tulbagh was struck by the most destructive earthquake in South Africa's recorded history in 1969, and its old Church Street was restored afterwards.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Shiraz, Chenin Blanc, Cap Classique.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Horseshoe valley; 1969 earthquake.</p>"]], "producers": ["Saronsberg", "Krone (Twee Jonge Gezellen)", "Waverley Hills", "Rijk's", "Fable Mountain", "Montpellier"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-vlottenburg"] = {"lede": "A ward south-west of Stellenbosch town around the Vlottenburg railway halt and the Eerste River, where cooling False Bay winds reach the vines in the afternoon.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chenin Blanc, Sauvignon Blanc"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Its old co-operative cellar is now Stellenbosch Hills, and the ward's Bordeaux varieties and Chenin Blanc benefit from afternoon sea breezes.</p><p>Alluvial and shale-derived soils on gentle slopes.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Structured Cabernet Sauvignon and Bordeaux blends, Pinotage, Chenin Blanc and Chardonnay.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Stellenbosch district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": ["Stellenbosch Hills (Vlottenburg cellar)", "Spier (nearby)"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-voor-paardeberg"] = {"lede": "The southern and eastern side ('voor', in front of) the Paardeberg, the granite massif between Paarl and the Swartland.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Shiraz, Chenin Blanc, Pinotage, Cinsault"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>Old dry-land bush vines of Chenin, Cinsault and Shiraz supply many of the Cape's new-wave winemakers.</p><p>Decomposed granite over clay, dry farmed; warm and windy.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style. This is a ward, the smallest level below a single vineyard.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Full Shiraz, Pinotage, Chenin Blanc and Rhône-style blends.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Ward in the Paarl district, Coastal Region region.</p>"]], "producers": ["Scali", "Vondeling", "Seven Springs (sources)"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
APXF["za-wellington"] = {"lede": "A district north of Paarl at the foot of the Hawequa mountains, about 45 minutes from Cape Town.", "facts": [["Category", "WO"], ["Recognized", ""], ["Main grapes", "Pinotage, Shiraz, Chenin Blanc, Cabernet Sauvignon"]], "sections": [["The place", "<p>It supplies more than 85 percent of South Africa's vine cuttings from its nurseries, and its 30 or so producers range from historic estates to Diemersfontein, which created 'coffee Pinotage' in 2001. Its five wards (Blouvlei, Bovlei, Groenberg, Limietberg and Mid-Berg River) were added recently.</p><p>Alluvial terraces along the Berg River and granite and sandstone foothills; snow on the peaks in winter, cooler nights than the coast.</p>"], ["The rules", "<p>A Wine of Origin production area. South Africa's Wine of Origin scheme came into force in 1973 and now operates under the Liquor Products Act of 1989, administered by the Wine and Spirit Board. The law defines a hierarchy of geographical units (such as Western Cape), regions, districts and wards, plus registered single vineyards; overarching units such as Cape Coast and Greater Cape allow blends across regions. A ward must have a clear influence of soil, climate and ecology on the wine, and its name must reflect a real geographic feature. 'Wine of Origin' with an area name guarantees that 100% of the grapes come from that area; a variety or vintage on the label requires 85%. The certification seal on the bottle confirms that the claims were checked and the wine passed tasting. The scheme sets no rules on grapes, yields or style.</p>"], ["History", "<p>Wellington produces most of South Africa's grapevine nursery material, and Huguenots farmed here from the late seventeenth century.</p>"], ["In the glass", "<p>Chenin Blanc, Shiraz, Pinotage and Cabernet.</p>"], ["For the exam", "<p>Vine nurseries; Chenin Blanc; Groenberg, Limietberg wards.</p>"]], "producers": ["Diemersfontein", "Bosman Family Vineyards", "Mont du Toit", "Andreas"], "sources": [["Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme", "https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme"], ["Wine Certification Authority: What we do", "https://wineauthority.org/what-we-do"], ["SAWIS: Wine of Origin Scheme (Liquor Products Act 60 of 1989), updated 2025", "https://www.sawis.co.za/winelaw/download/Wine_of_Origin_Scheme,_updated_05_2025.pdf"]], "checked": "2026-11-14"};
